Page-side modules (code that runs next to web pages, in their content process).

Add a file <name>.ts here (or <name>/index.ts). tools/build.mjs discovers it and bundles it into
actors/VitrePageChild.sys.mjs; there is nothing to register.

A module exports any of: events, onEvent(ctx, event), onMessage(ctx, name, data), onDestroy(ctx).
The full contract and the rules are in the header of ../page-api.ts. core.ts is the built-in example
(ping, and the scroll / pageshow / paint signals the window uses to re-sample the glass theme).
Built in: core (theme sampling signals), keys (Esc layers), pdf (the PDF viewer under the bar),
inset (pages start below the bar: the top strip, its skip rule and the header heuristic are in its header).

Settings: a page module reads its own setting straight from the vitre.* pref, in the content process:
  Services.prefs.getBoolPref(settingPref('pageInset'), true)      (settingPref: src/shared/settings.ts)
The read is synchronous, so it works before the first layout. VitreSettings installs every default on
the default branch before the first content process starts, and Gecko mirrors each pref change to the
content processes, so Services.prefs.addObserver(name, fn) in the page module hears the Settings panel
(or about:config) at once. Observe per document and remove the observer in onDestroy. Prefer boolean and
integer settings here: Gecko withholds some string prefs from web content processes, and nothing a page
process must not know belongs in a setting a page module reads.

Window side: b.page(tab).send(name, data), await b.page(tab).query(name, data),
b.on('page-message', (tab, name, data, from) => ...). See src/window/browser.ts.

Files starting with "_" are ignored; _generated.ts is written by the build.
